# tools/r/room_braid_witness.rish -- the single-stranded claim, pulled on rather than believed.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/room_braid_witness.rish
#
# WHY THIS RUNG. `foundations/20260823-204456_single-stranded.md` makes this tree's central
# architectural claim -- each module is about one thing, and "you can check it by trying to pull
# one part out" -- and the air row's threshold page repeats the test as its own sense: pull gently,
# and if other things move with it the strand was a braid. The claim stood in a foundation for
# eighteen days, was read on every air lap of that span, and NOTHING in the tree had ever performed
# the pull. A claim proven in almost nothing is the water row's shape one room over, and this is
# the air half of it.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN, all of it on metal.
#
#   Sixteen behaviors on real git repositories in a throwaway pen, by the control: an acyclic
#   three-room chain read as free, one link closing it read as a braid of three, that link lifted
#   returning the reading to free, an unimported link counted dead and closing no cycle, a link
#   imported only by ANOTHER link counted live, a same-room link counted as no edge at all, and an
#   empty corpus refusing rather than reading a clean zero. Every welcome is asserted as hard as
#   every refusal, because a refusal proven only in the passing direction cannot be told from an
#   instrument that always refuses -- which is not hypothetical here. The census's first draft
#   resolved its root from its own `$0` and so read the real tree from inside the pen; two legs
#   "passed" by matching this tree's own refusal, and only the failing direction exposed it.
#
#   The tree holds the shape. `braid_max` -- the largest set of rooms that cannot be lifted out
#   separately -- under a ceiling that only falls.
#
# WHY A CEILING RATHER THAN A GATE AT ZERO. Nine rooms stand in one component today, and asking
# the tree for zero would red every lap over a shape no single lap can repair. A ceiling refuses
# the ARRIVAL of a tenth room, which is the class a lap can actually cause, and rewards the repair
# by falling. Lower it when one lands.

let braid = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/r/room_braid_census_scan.sh"]
assert braid.ok else "room-braid: a room joined the braid -- read the census's own component lines"
assert braid.out contains "ceiling=9" else "room-braid: the ceiling moved -- a raise is a deliberate hand, and this one is meant only to fall"
assert braid.out contains "verdict=under_ceiling" else "room-braid: the census refused"
say "GREEN: room-braid -- the tree holds under its ceiling, and the pull is a measurement."

let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/r/room_braid_control.sh"]
assert control.ok else "room-braid: the control refused -- the census no longer answers a corpus whose answer is known"
assert control.out contains "fail=0" else "room-braid: a control leg failed"
assert control.out contains "an empty corpus refuses" else "room-braid: the empty-corpus leg is absent"
assert control.out contains "lifting the link returns it to acyclic" else "room-braid: the lift leg is absent"
say "GREEN: room-braid -- sixteen behaviors on planted trees, every refusal shown from both sides."
